WEBVTT

NOTE week07-clip2-zero-shot
NOTE Narration read by an AI-generated voice.
NOTE The clip has its own caption along the bottom of the frame, so these cues
NOTE are placed near the top to keep the two from overlapping.

1
00:00:00.150 --> 00:00:06.850 line:2 align:center
Once images and text share one space,
we can classify without any training.

2
00:00:06.850 --> 00:00:11.814 line:2 align:center
We write one text prompt for
each of the eight tissue classes,

3
00:00:11.814 --> 00:00:15.683 line:2 align:center
such as an H&E image of tumor stroma.

4
00:00:15.883 --> 00:00:19.315 line:2 align:center
The text encoder turns
each prompt into a vector.

5
00:00:19.315 --> 00:00:24.696 line:2 align:center
Together, the eight vectors act as
the weights of a linear classifier,

6
00:00:24.696 --> 00:00:28.950 line:2 align:center
written in text instead
of learned from labels.

7
00:00:29.150 --> 00:00:32.348 line:2 align:center
A new tile goes through the image encoder,

8
00:00:32.348 --> 00:00:38.987 line:2 align:center
its embedding is compared with every class
vector, and a softmax picks the class.

9
00:00:38.987 --> 00:00:44.160 line:2 align:center
BiomedCLIP gives this tumor
tile 0.57 for adenocarcinoma:

10
00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:46.750 line:2 align:center
correct, but not confident.

11
00:00:46.950 --> 00:00:50.635 line:2 align:center
On natural images this
works remarkably well.

12
00:00:50.635 --> 00:00:56.876 line:2 align:center
CLIP's best model reaches 76.2% on
ImageNet without a single ImageNet label,

13
00:00:56.876 --> 00:01:02.852 line:2 align:center
matching a ResNet-50 trained
on 1.28 million labeled images.

14
00:01:02.852 --> 00:01:07.883 line:2 align:center
The same paper notes it is weak
on lymph node tumor detection.

15
00:01:08.083 --> 00:01:12.486 line:2 align:center
On our 5,000 H&E tiles in 8 classes,

16
00:01:12.486 --> 00:01:17.887 line:2 align:center
general CLIP scores between 5.6 and 15.1%,

17
00:01:17.887 --> 00:01:24.147 line:2 align:center
depending only on the wording
of the prompt. Chance is 12.5%,

18
00:01:24.147 --> 00:01:27.217 line:2 align:center
so the worst prompt is below chance.

19
00:01:27.417 --> 00:01:32.463 line:2 align:center
BiomedCLIP, trained on figures and
captions from biomedical papers,

20
00:01:32.463 --> 00:01:38.563 line:2 align:center
does much better, at 34 to 39%.
That is still far from reliable,

21
00:01:38.563 --> 00:01:43.217 line:2 align:center
and the wording of the prompt
still changes the result.

22
00:01:43.417 --> 00:01:46.809 line:2 align:center
Now train a linear probe on
the same frozen embeddings,

23
00:01:46.809 --> 00:01:49.328 line:2 align:center
with only ten labeled tiles per class.

24
00:01:49.328 --> 00:01:55.149 line:2 align:center
It reaches 64% with CLIP
and 77% with BiomedCLIP.

25
00:01:55.149 --> 00:01:58.180 line:2 align:center
A few labels beat a clever prompt.
